# JavaScript, file size, and modularity

## Language and stack

- Write new code in plain JavaScript (`.js`). Do not add TypeScript (`.ts`, `.tsx`) or type-only files.
- Do not introduce frameworks (React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, Next.js, and similar). Use the language and the browser or Node APIs already in the project.
- A small existing tool such as a bundler may stay. Do not add a framework on top of it.

```js
// ❌ BAD — TypeScript and a component framework
export function Score({ value }: { value: number }) {
  return <p>{value}</p>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — plain JavaScript module
export function formatScore(value) {
  return String(value);
}
```

## File size

- Keep every file you create or edit at or under 300 lines.
- If a change would push a file past 300 lines, split it before finishing.

## Modularity

- One responsibility per module. Name the file after that responsibility.
- Extract a function when a block does a distinct job, or when the same logic appears twice.
- Keep public exports small. Hide helpers in the same file unless another module needs them.
- Pass data in through arguments. Avoid shared mutable state across modules.

```js
// ❌ BAD — input, rules, and drawing in one file
function tick(state, keys, ctx) { /* hundreds of mixed lines */ }

// ✅ GOOD — split by job
// input.js reads keys, physics.js updates state, draw.js paints the frame
```
